From 2a2d1261d12e3054b9e3f9e134f51048bb1ba1ad Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Andrey Korshunov <razor1702@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 17 Sep 2026 17:08:07 +0300
Subject: [PATCH] nl: offload on the outermost device of the chain first

bridger_nl_flow_offload_add() asks the flow's ingress device to take the
flower filter and only walks on to its offload chain when that add fails.
For a DSA user port the switch is asked first, and the conduit - the
device whose SoC programs the flow - only gets a chance when the switch
reports an error.

Flows between two ports of the same switch never reach this function,
device_match_phys_switch() skips them earlier, so an offloaded flow
always leaves the switch through its conduit anyway.

On a MediaTek MT7988 with a Motorcomm YT9224 the switch offloads ACL
rules only and cannot program a redirect to a Wi-Fi netdev. With the
previous commit the refusal becomes visible and the fallback works, but
the switch is still asked on every re-add, and one of those came back as
a success with no rule installed anywhere - after which the transfer
stayed on the CPU for the rest of the flow's life:

  Add flow on lan1: No child process
  Add flow on eth1: Success
  ...
  Add flow on lan1: Success        <- no filter on lan1 or eth1

Try the outermost device of the chain first and fall back inwards.
Measured with iperf3 from a wired PC to a 5 GHz client: the filters stay
on the conduit for the whole run, the ingress and egress netdev counters
stay at zero, ~1.75 Gbit/s over the air, 98% idle CPU.

Assisted-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Signed-off-by: Andrey Korshunov <razor1702@gmail.com>
Signed-off-by: Felix Fietkau <nbd@nbd.name>
---
 device.h | 19 +++++++++++++++++++
 nl.c     | 13 +++++++++++--
 2 files changed, 30 insertions(+), 2 deletions(-)

diff --git a/device.h b/device.h
index e64b9e9..b6b31e7 100644
--- a/device.h
+++ b/device.h
@@ -90,6 +90,25 @@ static inline struct bridge *device_get_br(struct device *dev)
 	return dev->br;
 }
 
+static inline unsigned int device_offload_chain_len(struct device *dev)
+{
+	unsigned int len = 0;
+
+	for (; dev; dev = dev->offload_dev)
+		len++;
+
+	return len;
+}
+
+static inline struct device *
+device_offload_chain_get(struct device *dev, unsigned int idx)
+{
+	for (; idx > 0; idx--)
+		dev = dev->offload_dev;
+
+	return dev;
+}
+
 static inline bool
 device_match_phys_switch(struct device *dev1, struct device *dev2)
 {
diff --git a/nl.c b/nl.c
index f11857f..1f16077 100644
--- a/nl.c
+++ b/nl.c
@@ -877,6 +877,7 @@ __bridger_nl_flow_offload_add(struct bridger_flow *flow, struct device *dev)
 int bridger_nl_flow_offload_add(struct bridger_flow *flow)
 {
 	struct device *dev;
+	unsigned int i;
 	int ifindex;
 	int ret = -1;
 
@@ -886,10 +887,18 @@ int bridger_nl_flow_offload_add(struct bridger_flow *flow)
 	if (flow->offload_ifindex)
 		bridger_nl_flow_offload_del(flow);
 
-	for (dev = flow->fdb_in->dev; dev; dev = dev->offload_dev) {
+	/*
+	 * A DSA user port cannot redirect to a device behind its conduit.
+	 * Flows between two ports of the same switch are skipped earlier, so
+	 * an offloaded flow always leaves the switch through its conduit.
+	 * Start at the lowest device of the chain and fall back upwards.
+	 */
+	for (i = device_offload_chain_len(flow->fdb_in->dev); i > 0; i--) {
+		dev = device_offload_chain_get(flow->fdb_in->dev, i - 1);
 		ifindex = device_ifindex(dev);
 		ret = __bridger_nl_flow_offload_add(flow, dev);
-		D("Add flow on %s: %s\n", dev->ifname, ret ? strerror(-ret) : "Success");
+		D("Add flow on %s: %s\n", dev->ifname,
+		  ret ? nl_geterror(ret) : "Success");
 		if (!ret)
 			break;
 	}
-- 
